John Henry Newman's Journey to the Light

- Newman was a pastor in the Church of England who got very sick while traveling in Italy
- When he was feverish and near death, he said, "I shall not die, for I have not sinned against the light"—meaning he had tried to follow God's truth
- On his way home, his ship got stuck at sea for a week with no wind to move it
- During that time, he wrote the famous hymn "Lead, Kindly Light," asking God to guide him step by step
- Back in England, he started a movement to help the church stay true to its ancient roots
- He studied the early Christian writers and realized the Catholic Church held the light of truth that had been passed down for centuries
- He believed the Church of England should be connected to the universal Catholic Church that Jesus started
- In order to remain with the Church of England, he needed to show that its doctrine was faithful to the doctrine of the larger church
- When people got angry at his ideas, he spent years studying and praying
- He realized that church teachings can develop and grow over time, like a light that gets brighter
- In 1845, he made the hard decision to leave his comfortable position and join the Catholic Church
- He wrote books explaining how the light of reason and of conscience led him to leave the Church of England
- After many years, the Pope made him a cardinal, honoring his faithfulness to following God's light